# skiroute

On-piste routing for ski resorts. Build a routable graph from
[OpenSkiData](https://openskidata.org/) and find the fastest way down
— or the way with the most vertical, or the way that avoids blacks.

```python
import skiroute

graph = skiroute.build_graph(
    gpkg_path="openskidata.gpkg",
    resort=skiroute.ResortFilter(localities=["Tignes", "Val d'Isère"]),
)

result = skiroute.route(
    graph,
    start=(6.985, 45.452),         # GPS coordinate (lon, lat)
    end=(6.907, 45.468),
    mode="prefer-easy",            # "prefer-easy" | "reds-if-needed" | "any-piste"
)

print(f"{result.total_minutes:.0f} min, {len(result.legs)} legs")
for leg in result.legs:
    print(f"  {leg.type}: {leg.name} ({leg.length_m:.0f}m)")
```

## Install

```bash
pip install skiroute
```

Or from source:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/caldvs/skiroute
cd skiroute
pip install -e .
```

You also need an OpenSkiData GeoPackage to build resort graphs.
Download it from [openskidata.org](https://openskidata.org/) — a single
file (~500 MB) covering every ski area in the world.

## What makes this different

There's lots of pathfinding code in the world. The interesting part of
`skiroute` is the cost model that turns generic Dijkstra into something
**skiers** actually want:

- **Difficulty-adjusted cost.** Every piste's cost is padded by a
  per-mode penalty. Under `prefer-easy`, a black run is many hundreds
  of seconds more expensive than its real ride time, so the router
  picks blues even when they're geographically longer.
- **Gap-bridging via skating edges.** OpenSkiData geometry has small
  gaps between segments. The builder creates synthetic "skating" edges
  between nodes within ~250 m, with cost tiers depending on the
  uphill gradient: flat (30 s), moderate 5–15 m gain (180 s), steep
  >15 m gain (600 s, strongly discouraged).
- **Lift-down handling.** Gondolas, funiculars, and cable cars produce
  a downhill edge too, with a penalty so the router prefers skiing.
  Under `prefer-easy` the penalty is discounted, so the router can ride
  a gondola down rather than ski an exposed black.
- **Most-skiing mode.** Subtracts a small bonus per metre of descent
  on each run edge — the router maximises vertical instead of
  minimising time. Useful for "I want to ski as much as possible on
  my way back to the village" routing.

## Three-layer API

```python
# --- Layer 1: build a graph for any resort in OpenSkiData ---
graph = skiroute.build_graph(
    gpkg_path="openskidata.gpkg",
    resort=skiroute.ResortFilter(
        localities=["Whistler"],
        # or: ski_area_pattern="Whistler%",
    ),
    config=skiroute.BuildConfig(),       # tunables; defaults are Alpine-tested
)
graph.save("whistler.json")
graph = skiroute.Graph.load("whistler.json")

# --- Layer 2: point-to-point routing ---
result = skiroute.route(
    graph, start=(lon, lat), end=(lon, lat), mode="any-piste",
)

# --- Layer 3: route to nearest of N destinations ---
result = skiroute.route_home(
    graph,
    start=(lon, lat),
    destinations=[
        skiroute.Destination("Whistler Village", lat=50.115, lon=-122.957, radius_m=600),
        skiroute.Destination("Creekside",        lat=50.096, lon=-122.974, radius_m=400),
    ],
    mode="reds-if-needed",
    objective="fastest",  # or "most-skiing"
)
```

## Routing algorithms

skiroute ships four algorithms with the same `(graph, src, tgt, cost_fn)`
contract — pick whichever fits your scale:

| Algorithm | Use when | Tradeoff |
|---|---|---|
| `algorithms.dijkstra` | Default; small-to-medium graphs | Visits ~half the graph |
| `algorithms.astar` | You can compute a goal-direction heuristic | Visits ~20 % fewer nodes than Dijkstra |
| `algorithms.bidirectional_dijkstra` | Larger graphs | Visits half what Dijkstra does on geographic graphs |
| `contraction_hierarchies.query` | You want microsecond queries on large graphs | One-time preprocessing pass |

For a 3,300-node 16-resort mega-network: Dijkstra visits 1,400 nodes per
query; CH visits 67. See `examples/ch_bench.py`.

## Stitching multiple resorts

Real resorts can be combined into a synthetic mega-network:

```python
from skiroute.stitch import Connector, stitch_graphs

tignes = skiroute.build_graph(gpkg, resort=skiroute.ResortFilter(
    ski_area_pattern="%Tignes - Val%",
), destinations=tignes_villages)

three_v = skiroute.build_graph(gpkg, resort=skiroute.ResortFilter(
    ski_area_pattern="%Trois Vallées%",
), destinations=three_v_villages)

mega = stitch_graphs(
    [tignes, three_v],
    connectors=[
        Connector("Grande Motte", "Cime Caron",
                  kind="cable_car", name="Vanoise Express"),
    ],
)
```

## Caveats

- **Cost constants are tuned on Tignes / Val d'Isère.** Sensible Alpine
  defaults, but bigger or different resorts may want to override
  `BuildConfig.max_gap_distance_m`, `lift_time_by_type`, and the
  skating-cost tiers.
- **OpenSkiData coverage varies.** Major resorts are complete; smaller
  ones may have missing geometry — use the `overrides_path` mechanism
  to patch.
- **No off-piste routing.** Edges are built only from runs marked
  `downhill` or `connection` in OpenSkiData.

## Data source

[OpenSkiData](https://openskidata.org/) publishes a global GeoPackage
of every ski area in the world, derived from OpenStreetMap. Download
the latest release and pass the path to `build_graph()`.

## Examples

The `examples/` directory has runnable scripts:

- `tignes_quickstart.py` — build Tignes / Val d'Isère, route a few queries
- `ch_bench.py` — benchmark Dijkstra / A\* / Bidirectional / CH on Tignes
- `ch_diagram.py` — generate an HTML visualisation of the CH structure
- `api_server.py` — FastAPI HTTP wrapper (legacy `ski-home-api` code,
  retained as a reference deployment)

